ment database is very suited for  storing shopping carts since shopping  carts can be easily indexed by user IDs  and you don't need the power of  relational database here cloud fire  store is a great fit for this purpose  it's managed and serve unless no sequel  document database so let's consider that  for our target architecture  now comes the biggest piece migrating  the actual shopping cart feature  assuming ice cream theory can schedule a  maintenance for their website wait wait  wait I don't know when is downtime for  ice creams I know I can eat ice creams  in the middle of the night I know let's  say that they can do scheduled  maintenance first they would need to  create a new micro service that can call  the inventory system and uses cloud fire  sword implement a shopping cart API then  create a script that extracts shopping  cart entries from the legacy shopping  cart system and writes them the cloud  fire store this  group needs to be written in a way that  it can be rerun as many times as needed  but only copies of shopping carts that  have been changed since last time then  create a script that does the opposite  which is it copies shopping carts from  cloud fire store back to the legacy  subsystem in case they need to rollback  the migration then expose the shopping  cart API with Apogee they would need to  prepare and test the modification to the  front-end and payment systems so they  can call the new shopping cart system  again comes the time to run the data  migration script we prepared earlier so  all the data is in sync and we do not  lose any shopping card records in the  process at this point they need to put  their website in maintenance mode and  rerun the data migration script deploy  the test and modifications done to the  front end in the payment system on the  legacy production system once done  they're ready to disable the maintenance  mode in the website and with that the  shopping cart feature of the ice cream  theory website is now microservice  hosted on Google Cloud the hardest step  of this process is where they had to  make changes to the legacy payment and  front-end system but that gets easier as  they migrate more and more features to  micro services because more and more the  dependencies will already be micro  services as such they're more loosely  coupled than in monolithic application  and are easier to modify and deploy for  more information about alternative  migration approaches check out the  article written by marco and Te'o well  there you have it if you are looking to  migrate a monolithic application to  micro services you've got a small taste  of the steps involved through the ice  cream theories useless stay tuned for  the next episode where we will talk  about deciding what to migrate first  until then try the end-to-end migration  of an e-commerce website in the codelab  link below that's all for today I'll get  cooking in cloud if you like what we're  cooking here and like to see more  recipes don't forget to Like and  subscribe to our Channel  [Music]  you

